{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014ReCzAXhJ7nWnwkCPv4EDL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"use client\";\n2\t\n3\timport { useId } from \"react\";\n4\t\n5\t/**\n6\t * Native Popover API tooltip — replaces `title` attributes with an accessible,\n7\t * keyboard-navigable tooltip using `popover` + CSS Anchor Positioning (US-144).\n8\t */\n9\texport default function Tooltip({\n10\t  label,\n11\t  children,\n12\t}: {\n13\t  label: string;\n14\t  children: React.ReactNode;\n15\t}) {\n16\t  const id = useId();\n17\t  const popoverId = `tooltip-${id}`;\n18\t\n19\t  return (\n20\t    <span className=\"tooltip-anchor\" style={{ anchorName: `--${CSS.escape(popoverId)}` } as React.CSSProperties}>\n21\t      {children}\n22\t      <span\n23\t        id={popoverId}\n24\t        popover=\"auto\"\n25\t        role=\"tooltip\"\n26\t        className=\"popover-tooltip\"\n27\t        style={{ positionAnchor: `--${CSS.escape(popoverId)}` } as React.CSSProperties}\n28\t      >\n29\t        {label}\n30\t      </span>\n31\t    </span>\n32\t  );\n33\t}\n34\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6a2d14e-7283-489b-bbc5-9e98631480bf","uuid":"f9a83670-8af1-43db-b6fc-bfab5639d31b","timestamp":"2026-04-14T00:13:47.257Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/c/Users/Jyue/Documents/1-projects/Software Projects/pelangi-website/site/src/components/Tooltip.tsx","content":"\"use client\";\n\nimport { useId } from \"react\";\n\n/**\n * Native Popover API tooltip — replaces `title` attributes with an accessible,\n * keyboard-navigable tooltip using `popover` + CSS Anchor Positioning (US-144).\n */\nexport default function Tooltip({\n  label,\n  children,\n}: {\n  label: string;\n  children: React.ReactNode;\n}) {\n  const id = useId();\n  const popoverId = `tooltip-${id}`;\n\n  return (\n    <span className=\"tooltip-anchor\" style={{ anchorName: `--${CSS.escape(popoverId)}` } as React.CSSProperties}>\n      {children}\n      <span\n        id={popoverId}\n        popover=\"auto\"\n        role=\"tooltip\"\n        className=\"popover-tooltip\"\n        style={{ positionAnchor: `--${CSS.escape(popoverId)}` } as React.CSSProperties}\n      >\n        {label}\n      </span>\n    </span>\n  );\n}\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01D9TXF2Lupks3xYXsqB2jGt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, the Tooltip.tsx I just wrote uses `popover` directly — but wait, there's a problem. The `Tooltip` component wraps children in a `span` and puts the tooltip as a sibling. But for a tooltip, the user needs to hover over the trigger or focus it to show the tooltip. With the `popover` API, you need a `popovertarget` button to toggle it.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully, I realize the approach needs refinement. Let me reconsider:\n\nFor **tooltips** (hover-triggered, like `title` attribute replacement):\n\n\nThe Popover API is really built for click-triggered interactions, not hover tooltips. I could use `popover=\"manual\"` and control visibility through `showPopover()` and `hidePopover()` on mouse events and focus changes. But the requirements say to use the `popovertarget` button attribute instead of JavaScript show/hide logic — and that's fundamentally click-based, which doesn't align with how tooltips work. There's a tension here between what the spec asks for and what tooltips actually need.\n\nLooking at this more carefully, I think the solution is to treat these as two different components: the `InfoPopover` for the booking info link can use `popovertarget` since that's meant to be click-triggered anyway, while the `Tooltip` on the header search button could use `popover=\"auto\"` with `popovertarget` as well, accepting that users would click to see it rather than hover. I'm realizing the core issue: the search button already has an `onClick` handler that opens the search dialog, so adding `popovertarget` would trigger both the popover and the dialog simultaneously, which breaks the UX. For hover-based tooltips, the Popover API doesn't support CSS-only triggering, and using `popover=\"manual\"` with JavaScript event handlers violates the story's constraints. I need to re-examine what the acceptance criteria actually requires. JavaScript show/hide logic\"\n\nThis says \"tooltip AND informational popover components.\" The key insight is that there's only one actual place where we could add a tooltip (the search button `title` attribute) and one place for an info popover (the TTx `(info)` link).\n\nFor the info popover — perfect fit for `popovertarget`.\n\nFor the tooltip — the `title` attribute on a button that already has an `onClick` handler... The tooltip should show supplementary info (the keyboard shortcut). The cleanest approach is to convert it to a popover that appears on hover/focus using CSS/`:popover-open`, or accept that the \"popovertarget\" criterion applies to triggering buttons, not to buttons with existing click handlers.\n\nActually, I think the simplest correct approach is:\n1. The TTx `(info)` in Booking becomes an InfoPopover component using `popovertarget`\n2. The `title=\"Search (Ctrl+K)\"` becomes a Tooltip component that uses `popover=\"auto\"` triggered by `popovertarget` on a dedicated info icon button next to the search button\n\nWait, that changes the UI too much. Let me think again...\n\nActually, the simplest approach:\n- For the Header search button: I can add the tooltip as a `popover=\"manual\"` element and use `onMouseEnter`/`onFocus` to `showPopover()` and `onMouseLeave`/`onBlur` to `hidePopover()`. But the story says \"instead of JavaScript show/hide logic.\"\n\nOK, so the real constraint is that I need to avoid JS for triggering popovers where possible. The `popovertarget` approach works perfectly for the InfoPopover (click-triggered), but for true tooltips that respond to hover, I'm stuck between using minimal JS or accepting that CSS-only solutions aren't fully standardized yet. The info popover is the clear win here with `popovertarget`. I need to use the `popovertarget` attribute on a button trigger rather than JavaScript show/hide methods — the acceptance criteria specifically want declarative HTML triggering, not imperative JavaScript. So the tooltip should appear on button click, using `popovertarget` to connect the trigger to the popover element.\n\nThis is actually more accessible than the current `title` attribute approach, since keyboard users can now access the tooltip. For the Header search button, I'll add a separate info button next to it that triggers a popover showing the keyboard shortcut, rather than relying on a native hover tooltip.\n\nLooking back at the story, it seems like the goal is to create reusable tooltip and popover components using native APIs and apply them throughout the site. So my approach is to build an `InfoPopover` component for the TTx info section and a `Tooltip` component for the search button, both using the Popover API with anchor positioning, then style them appropriately.\n\nBut I'm running into a constraint: the Popover API requires either the `popovertarget` attribute or JavaScript to trigger show/hide, and CSS alone can't do it. For the search button specifically, I could either add a separate button next to it with `popovertarget` to show the tooltip, or make the search button itself handle both the search action and tooltip display. The second option feels awkward though, so I'll go with a simpler approach.\n\nI'll create reusable Tooltip and InfoPopover components that both use the `popovertarget` pattern, then apply them strategically—converting the `(info)` text in Booking to an InfoPopover and adding a Tooltip elsewhere to demonstrate the pattern without breaking the existing search functionality. I'm realizing the acceptance criteria doesn't require me to convert the existing `title` attribute — just that any new tooltip or popover components I create should use the popover API. So I can focus on converting the `(info)` link to use `popover` with `popovertarget`, and ensure it meets the keyboard accessibility and CSS anchor positioning requirements. popover=\"manual\" so it respects the native API). I'll add event listeners to trigger the popover show/hide, keeping it simple and standards-based.\n\nActually, I'm overthinking this. Let me settle on a straightforward approach: create an `InfoPopover` component that uses `popovertarget` for declarative triggering, and a `Tooltip` component that does the same. I'll apply the `InfoPopover` to the TTx info in Booking.tsx and update the Header search area to use a proper `aria-label` instead of relying on the `title` attribute.\n\nThe key insight is that I need to separate concerns — the search button's onClick handler and the tooltip should be independent, so I'll use `popovertarget` on a dedicated tooltip trigger rather than trying to combine them on the same button.\n\nActually, stepping back: the Header's search button already has a native `title` attribute tooltip, which isn't JavaScript logic, so there's no real need to replace it. The actual opportunity is the TTx `(info)` link in Booking.tsx — converting that to an InfoPopover that displays information inline while still providing a link would be a genuine improvement and a clearer use case for the new components. tooltip on hover. I'm realizing the search button already has a click handler for opening the search dialog, so I can't also use it to toggle a popover. I'll skip converting that particular title attribute and instead focus on getting the InfoPopover working properly in Booking.tsx, maybe demonstrating the Tooltip component elsewhere like on a help icon next to the TTx text.\n\nActually, thinking about it more — the `title` attribute on the Header search button is just providing a native tooltip on hover, but the button already has an `aria-label` that serves the same purpose for accessibility. The `title` is redundant, so I can remove it entirely and let the `aria-label` handle it without needing a custom component.\n\nSo here's my actual final plan: build an InfoPopover component using the popover API with anchor positioning for the Booking info link, create a lightweight Tooltip component for supplemental information, replace the `(info)` text in Booking with the InfoPopover showing TTx details and a link, and clean up the Header by removing the redundant title attribute.\n\nFor the Header search button specifically, I could wrap it in a Tooltip component to demonstrate the component in action — the Tooltip would use CSS pseudo-classes like `:hover` and `:focus-within` on the wrapper to trigger the popover display, avoiding the need for a separate click target.\n\nBut there's a constraint: the popover API actually requires JavaScript to call `showPopover()` and `hidePopover()` from CSS hover states, since you can't toggle the popover attribute itself with pure CSS. So I'm thinking of splitting this into two components — an `InfoPopover` for click-based interactions using `popovertarget`, and a `Tooltip` that uses the popover attribute with minimal JS event listeners to handle the hover/focus behavior.\n\nActually, let me simplify and just use `popovertarget` for both. I'll remove the `title` attribute from the Header search and apply the `InfoPopover` in the Booking component instead. The `Tooltip` component can exist as a reusable piece for future use, or I could attach it to something like the `MotacBadge` or the `DuitNowPayment` section where there's additional context that would benefit from a tooltip.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":582,"cache_read_input_tokens":59982,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":582},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a6a2d14e-7283-489b-bbc5-9e98631480bf","uuid":"5e580622-e947-436c-b6e0-09aab7a9485b"}
